(D1 - Discrepancy 1 repro: LANGUAGE.md:39 says "Every statement ends with
 a period", but the compiler does not require one, and a missing period
 does not fail - it silently changes which clause the next statement
 belongs to, differently for `If` than for `While`.
 Line 1-2: two statements with no periods at all, even run together on one
 line - both run.
 Line 4-6: `While ... , Increment x` with no period; `Print "in loop"` is
 ABSORBED into the loop body (it prints three times).
 Line 9-11: the same shape with `If`; `Print "after if"` is NOT absorbed -
 it prints even though the condition is false, so the newline ended the
 `If` where it did not end the `While`.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox D1.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   one
   two
   in loop
   in loop
   in loop
   after if
   end
)
Print "one" Print "two".
a number called x is 0.
While x is less than 3, Increment x
Print "in loop".
a number called y is 1.
If y is equal to 2 then, Print "then branch"
Print "after if".
Print "end".
